title: Generic panel commands
tags: Commands Keybindings
kind: reference
aliases: generic-commands cursor-commands provide
summary: One command for every panel that can do it — cursor:next self moves the cursor of whatever panel has the focus — run on functions the panel provides with metafolder.provide.

Some commands do the same thing in every panel that shows rows under a cursor:
moving to the next row, to the first, finding a row by name. They are not one
command per panel type (`trash:next`, `log:next`…) but ''one command'', which
the shell runs on a few functions the panel provides:

|!Command |!Does |!Needs |
|<<cmd "cursor:next">> |the next row; on the last row loaded, loads more first (`more`) |`index`, `count`, `select` |
|<<cmd "cursor:prev">> |the previous row |`index`, `count`, `select` |
|<<cmd "cursor:first">> |the first row |`count`, `select` |
|<<cmd "cursor:last">> |the last row loaded |`count`, `select` |
|<<cmd "cursor:find">> `select` |the row named in the command input ([[Jumping to an entry]]) |`entries`, `select` |
|<<cmd "cursor:find">> `walk` |the same, going into what can be gone into and asking again |`entries`, `select`, `descend` |
|<<cmd "cursor:descend">> |into the row under the cursor: a directory, a node, a label |`index`, `descend` |
|<<cmd "cursor:parent">> |up one level: the parent directory, node or label |`parent` |
|<<cmd "cursor:root">> |to the top: the root directory, a forest's roots |`root` |
|<<cmd "view:refresh">> |reads what the panel shows again |`refresh` |
|<<cmd "view:zoom">> `in`/`out`/`reset` |zooms the whole panel, kept per workspace |`zoom` — every panel has it: the shell provides it |

With no row under the cursor, `next` and `prev` both land on the first row, and
none of them moves past either end. An empty list is left alone.

!! Which panel

The first argument is ''required'': the panel the command acts on.

* `self` is the panel that has the focus: `cursor:next self` is what <<key "cursor:next self" j>> runs.
* A panel type is that panel in the workspace, focused or not, on screen or not — mounted first if it has to be, like a panel's own command. In the [[file panel]], the arrows run `cursor:next metarecord-list`: the list beside it moves, and the preview follows.

A script names the panel it drives (`mf gui command "cursor:next trash"`), so
what it does does not depend on where the focus happens to be.

A panel that lacks a function the command needs answers with an error:
`cursor:next is not implemented for the help panel`. A ''key'' bound to the
command does not get that far: a binding fires only where its command applies,
so in a panel without a cursor the key passes through as if it were not bound,
and the help scrolls with the arrows as before ([[Keybindings]]).

!! Providing the functions

A panel type hands the shell its functions once, at mount
([[Writing a panel type]]):

```
metafolder.provide({
  index: () => cursorIndex,         // the cursor's row, -1 when there is none
  count: () => rows.length,         // how many rows (loaded, for a paged list)
  select: (i) => moveCursorTo(i),   // always called with 0 <= i < count()
  entries: () => rows.map((r) => ({ name: r.name, label: `${r.name} — ${r.path}` })),
});
```

|!Function |!Is |
|`index()` |the row under the cursor, -1 when there is none |
|`count()` |how many rows there are — loaded ones, for a paged list |
|`select(i)` |puts the cursor on row `i`, which the shell keeps within `0..count()-1` |
|`more()` |loads more rows; asked by `cursor:next` on the last row loaded (the [[metarecord-list panel]]) |
|`entries()` |the rows `cursor:find` searches, as displayed now: a `name`, an optional `label` the candidates show and the typed terms match, and an optional `index` — the row `select` gets when the entries skip some (the file manager's `.` and `..`) |
|`descend(i)` |goes into row `i` — a directory, a node, a label — and says whether it could (a file, an operation, cannot be gone into) |
|`leaf(i)` |whether row `i` has nothing below it, for a panel whose `descend` goes into a leaf too: the tree explorer, where an empty node is where children are added. A walk stops on a leaf — without `leaf`, on what `descend` refuses |
|`refresh()` |reads what the panel shows again |
|`parent()` |goes up one level |
|`root()` |goes to the top level |

Every function is optional; a panel provides what it can do, and a later call
adds to what an earlier one provided. Each panel's note in the [[Panel]] catalog
lists what it provides.

!! Why one command

Seven list panels each had their own `next`, `prev`, `first`, `last` and, but
one, `find` — some thirty commands doing one thing, each with its note — and
every key carried one binding per panel: `j` alone was eight lines of
`keybindings.toml`, and moving it meant editing all eight. The code behind
them was one line each (`select(cursorIndex + 1)`): what differed from panel to
panel was what a row is, never what moving does. That part is now the panel's
functions, and the moving is written once, in the shell
(`frontend/src/lib/panelFunctions.ts`).
